      "severity": "blocker",
      "claim": "The acceptance criterion's fourth `wrong_versions` entry — \"`_drive` increments `consecutive_aborts` by `2` for an unmarked raise and leaves it unchanged for a marked one\" — is not actually caught by the witness this spec specifies. In the exact scenario the Problem section builds (candidate 1 raises a marked exception, candidate 2 raises an unmarked one, candidate 3 never runs), the correct implementation's tally goes 0 -> 1 -> 2 and this wrong implementation's tally goes 0 -> 0 -> 2: both reach 2 at the same point, before candidate 3's breaker check, so every assertion the criterion names (reason == INFRASTRUCTURE, call order limited to the first two ids, a raised line for each, a package command line only for the first) comes out identical under both implementations. The wrong_versions entry's own wording half-admits this (\"reaches the same tally ... only by coincidence, and a second unmarked raise overshoots it\"), but the witness built here uses exactly the one-marked-plus-one-unmarked case where the coincidence holds, not a scenario with a second unmarked raise that would expose the overshoot. The spec names a wrong build its own witness would pass rather than fail.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": ".saffron/specs/SA-0317-a-package-raise-s-mark-still-counts-toward-the-breaker-its-own-comment-implied-otherwise.md",
      "line": null,
      "fixes": "witness"
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The second `wrong_versions` entry — \"`_promote_in_flight` resets `consecutive_aborts` to `0` whenever it closes a row `INFRASTRUCTURE` for a marked raise, so the breaker starts counting over from the next candidate\" — describes an edit with no reachable effect on which candidates run. `_promote_in_flight` takes no `consecutive_aborts` parameter at base and is called once, only after `_drive`'s loop (or, for a stack batch, both `_drive` calls) has already finished deciding every candidate for that pass; the follow-up `_drive` call takes its own `consecutive_aborts` directly from the prior call's return value, not from anything `_promote_in_flight` touches. A reset there would be a no-op with respect to \"the next candidate,\" since no candidate decision is left to make by the time it runs. This doesn't threaten the acceptance criterion the way the fourth wrong_versions entry does, but it is a sloppy illustrative example: an edit incapable of producing the regression it names.",
      "criterion": 1,
